S_FMT must write the accepted format back to userspace

VIDIOC_S_FMT is _IOWR, so the driver is obliged to rewrite struct
v4l2_format. The original handler only read the caller's
width/height/pixelformat into dev and mipi_top and never touched
bytesperline / sizeimage, so whatever the caller passed in came straight
back out.

ustreamer's _capture_open_format() always fills in
bytesperline = us_align_size(width, 32) << 1 (a packed-YUV assumption), so
when capturing NV16 it believes the Y plane stride is 3840 rather than 1920:

  - the top half of the picture is squashed to 2x height (every row skips one)
  - by row 540 the chroma pointer has run past the end of the chroma plane
    and reads the zero padding -> solid green
  - further down it reads past the mmap -> SIGSEGV, and kvmd restarts the
    streamer every 3 seconds

G_FMT / TRY_FMT already go through fill_pix_format(); S_FMT should match.

---

--- a/linux-rtk/drivers/media/platform/rtk_hdmirx/rtd129x/hdmirx_video_dev.c
+++ b/linux-rtk/drivers/media/platform/rtk_hdmirx/rtd129x/hdmirx_video_dev.c
@@ -491,6 +491,21 @@
 		HDMIRX_INFO(" ioctl VIDIOC_S_FMT,output v(%u) h(%u) color(%u) pitch(%u)",
 			mipi_top.h_output_len, mipi_top.v_output_len,
 			mipi_top.output_color, mipi_top.pitch);
+
+		/*
+		 * VIDIOC_S_FMT is _IOWR: the driver must write back the format it
+		 * accepted. This handler only read, so the caller's bytesperline /
+		 * sizeimage came back unchanged.
+		 *
+		 * ustreamer always fills bytesperline = align(width,32) * 2 (a
+		 * packed-YUV assumption), so it believes NV16's Y stride is 3840
+		 * rather than 1920: the top half is squashed to 2x height, the
+		 * bottom turns green past the chroma plane, then it SIGSEGVs.
+		 *
+		 * G_FMT / TRY_FMT already use fill_pix_format(); S_FMT should match.
+		 */
+		fill_pix_format(&fmt->fmt.pix, mipi_top.h_output_len,
+				mipi_top.v_output_len, dev->outfmt);
 		break;
 	}
 	case VIDIOC_S_PARM:
